{} "7 – 11 August 1928"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/286"} "Krachtsportgebouw, Amsterdam"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/21743"} "24 from 24 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "Single elimination tournament."]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The previous year’s European champion did not compete in this division in Amsterdam. Jakob Domgörgen of Germany was not selected, as the European featherweight champion " [:a {:href "/athletes/3598"} "Franz Dübbers"] " had moved up a weight division. Dübbers was considered as one of the favorites, along with the American national champion " [:a {:href "/athletes/8685"} "Steve Halaiko"] " and Sweden’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/8141"} "Gunnar Berggren"] ", but was put out of the tournament by the unheralded Argentine " [:a {:href "/athletes/3315"} "Pascual Bonfiglio"] ". The deaf-mute Italian " [:a {:href "/athletes/4151"} "Carlo Orlandi"] " had the most impressive performance of the quarter-finals, dispatching " [:a {:href "/athletes/7878"} "Cecil Bissett"] " of Rhodesia inside the first round, and consolidated this effort by outpointing " [:a {:href "/athletes/1747"} "Hans Nielsen"] " of Denmark in the semi-final stage. The second semi-final saw Halaiko and Berggren enter the ring together. Halaiko was given the decision, which was unpopular with the Swedish fans and led to brief clashes between Swedes and Americans in the arena. The final was another close contest, the judges this time siding with the Italian over the American."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Both finalists went on to long pro careers. Orlandi had spells as national lightweight and welterweight champion, and was also European champion at lightweight. A crushing defeat to the Puerto Rican Pedro Montañez in 1935 left him in the hospital for three months and his career lost momentum after that, although he did record nearly 100 victories. New Yorker Halaiko also had over a century of professional bouts."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/4151"} "Carlo Orlandi"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ITA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/ITA.png"}] "ITA"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/8685"} "Steve Halaiko"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/8141"} "Gunnar Berggren"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/SWE"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/SWE.png"}] "SWE"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Bronze"} "Bronze"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "4"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/1747"} "Hans Nielsen"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/DEN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/DEN.png"}] "DEN"]] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "=5"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/4691"} "David Baan"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NED"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NED.png"}] "NED"]] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "=5"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/7878"} "Cecil Bissett"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ZIM"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-al